WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
WILFRED MCCLAY: An
incalculable advantage

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.510
that the Americans had I
can express in two words--

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.830
George Washington.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.050
Declaring independence
was the easy part.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.130
Defeating the mightiest
military power in the world,

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.350
that was another matter.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.630
The United States,
such as they were--

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.610
the disunited United States--

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.230
went into this conflict
with huge and fairly obvious

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.700
disadvantages.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.030
But all was not lost because
the Americans did enjoy

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:41.430
certain very real advantages.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.510
For one thing, they had
only to play defense.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.780
They needed not to
win every battle.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.600
They needed not
to win any battles

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.420
so long as they were able
to keep the war going long

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.500
enough to exhaust the patience
and willingness to fight

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.030
of the British opponent.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.870
Another advantage, an
incalculable advantage,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.800
that the Americans had I
can express in two words--

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.480
George Washington.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.390
They had the person of George
Washington, a man of such fine

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:15.440
and admired--

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.170
widely, universally admired
character, and military skills,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.840
political savvy.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.860
He commanded the loyalty
of nearly all Americans,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.930
and thus served as a
unifying force when

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.460
he was made commander in chief
of the Revolutionary American

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.860
forces.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.190
He signaled his willingness
to play this role

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.440
by appearing at the
Second Continental

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.400
Congress in Philadelphia
wearing his military uniform.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.770
So he ready to play,
and from June 1775 on

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.970
was commander in chief
of the Continental Army.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.180
He received the position and
accepted it on the condition

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.510
that he received no pay for it.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.910
That was the kind of guy
George Washington was.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:11.370
He was charismatic,
courageous, selfless, tireless.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.240
He was not a world-class
intellect like Jefferson.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.460
But he was always
learning and had

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.150
a great deal of practical
and prudential wisdom.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.700
The greatest challenge
that Washington would face

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.610
was simply one of recruiting
and holding together

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.050
a credible army.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.210
This was not an easy job.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.480
There was a sense of fragility
about the whole enterprise

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.960
so that the morale
and willingness

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.500
to serve of soldiers
waxed and waned depending

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.970
on circumstances in the
field and weather conditions,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.330
which were very important
in the American Revolution.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:52.350
In August of 1776, not
long after the Declaration,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.490
he had 28,000 men
under his command.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:01.230
By December, that number
had shrunk to 3,000.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.630
What had happened was
that those troops have

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:09.270
been driven from New York
City by British regulars,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:14.470
back and back and back until
they ended up in Pennsylvania.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.650
The army's morale was bad.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.510
It was always unstable.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:24.750
It was always hostage to events,
to successive peaks and valleys

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.250
and consequent hope and
despair, depending on how

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.200
the war effort was going.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.550
Washington through all of
this had to keep a cool head.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.000
He had to not be to
influenced either

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.960
by the giddiness of success
or the despondency of failure.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.800
As it turned out that
winter, 1776 to '77,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.980
was an exceptionally bad winter.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.560
And it made for a gloomy
time of discouragement.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.840
Fortunately for
the patriot cause,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.430
Tom Paine chose that time to
write yet another contribution

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.890
to the war effort, a
series of pamphlets

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.290
called The American Crisis.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.500
Which the most famous lines
from that, which some of you

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.430
may have heard, go like
this at the beginning.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.450
He says, "These are the
times that try men's souls.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.850
The summer soldier and
the sunshine patriot

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.550
will, in this crisis,
shrink from the service

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.040
of his country; but
he that stands it

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.910
now deserves the love and
thanks of man and woman."

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:38.330
Yes, he conceded that
things look very bad.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:42.080
But the harder of the conflict,
the more glorious the triumph.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.220
Paine put it just the
way Washington would have

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.600
liked to have been able to.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.920
And George Washington
ordered that Paine's words

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.070
from The American Crisis
be read to his troops who

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.000
were suffering through
this bitter winter.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.022
And they had the desired
effect of rousing them

00:05:02.022 --> 00:05:05.370
to a new burst of
fighting spirit.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:10.670
And then on Christmas night of
1776, Washington struck back.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.100
He led a force through
snow and sleet,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.840
across the Delaware River.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.940
We've all seen the
painting of that,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.090
which is not exactly
historically accurate, but who

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.540
cares?

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.510
It's a beautiful painting.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.140
And he surprised at
dawn a sleeping force

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:32.300
of Hessians, the hated Hessians,
at Trenton in New Jersey.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.560
A week later, his forces had a
similar triumph at Princeton.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.640
Two small victories
brought reason for hope

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.000
that they could be auguries
of good things to come.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.600
It appeared the American forces
would not be defeated quickly,

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.820
as the British had hoped.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.270
